{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/AutoResetEvent.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/AutoResetEvent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"auto reset event auto reset event","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/AutoResetEvent.md","fileName":"AutoResetEvent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"AutoResetEvent","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Synchronization and Thread Coordination]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Synchronization and Thread Coordination","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/AutoResetEvent.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/AutoResetEvent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"auto reset event auto reset event","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/AutoResetEvent.md","fileName":"AutoResetEvent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"AutoResetEvent","heading":"AutoResetEvent","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An `AutoResetEvent` is a signaling primitive that releases one waiting thread when signaled and then returns automatically to the nonsignaled state. A signal that arrives with no waiter remains available for one future waiter rather than accumulating an arbitrary count.\n\nIt models a handoff rather than ownership of a critical section. Because only one waiter proceeds per stored signal, it differs from a manual-reset gate and from a semaphore whose count can represent several available permits.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Synchronization and Thread Coordination","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/AutoResetEvent.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/AutoResetEvent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"auto reset event aut